<!-- cell: title-page id="e5f412d2-4aab-4f7c-acf9-1c867936ca27" title="Hostage" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="c679946e-8447-41e8-ba2c-e87c794fd36b" -->

Eleven thousand Swarm ships hung above the gas giant Oro like frost on a window, and the flagship Meridian was the only human vessel close enough to see them with the naked eye.

Captain Ilse Varn watched them from the bridge, hands clasped behind her back so her crew would not see them tremble. Behind her, the colonies of the Oro moons held nine million people and no fleet worth the name.

"Their envoy is docking," said First Officer Dace. "Captain, we have every torpedo on the ship locked onto that shuttle. One word."

"No," said Varn.

The envoy Th'raan came aboard in a column of cold mist, tall and many-jointed, its voice arriving a second late through the translator. It spoke on the bridge, in front of everyone.

"Three of your ships fired on our nursery fleet at Kesh," it said. "Seven hundred of our young did not wake. You say it was a mistake. We say: a mistake is a word. We require a guarantee."

"What guarantee would satisfy you?" asked Varn.

"None that your kind has ever offered."

Dace leaned close. "Let me end this, Captain. While it's standing here."

Varn thought of her daughter on Oro's third moon, who believed her mother could fix anything.

"Then I offer something new," she said. "Me. I will come with you as your hostage, for as long as the peace holds. If one human ship fires on yours again, you may do with me as you like."

The bridge went silent. Th'raan's many eyes closed and opened, slowly, one pair after another.

"You would leave your ship," it said.

"I would leave everything."

"Captain," said Dace, "I won't allow it."

Varn took the command key from around her neck and pressed it into his hand. "You're relieved, Commander. You have the Meridian. Keep the guns cold."

He stared at the key. For a long moment she did not know which way he would turn. Then he straightened and saluted, and his voice did not break when he gave the order to stand down weapons.

Varn walked into the mist beside the envoy. Behind her, on every screen, the frost above Oro began to thin as ship after ship turned away into the dark.

Three years later the peace still held, and every evening, in a garden built for her on a Swarm world, Ilse Varn wrote a letter to a daughter who was no longer a child.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="5d934ce1-c054-452a-8291-3a0382461b74" title="Captain Ilse Varn" words="33-53, 193-208, 345-352" -->

Commander of the flagship Meridian, who hides her trembling hands and offers herself as a hostage to keep nine million colonists alive.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="b707c421-bec9-41a3-8a78-a6d8afa236b9" title="Th'raan" words="97-119, 174-180, 252-262" -->

The envoy of the alien Swarm, many-jointed and many-eyed, who demands a guarantee no human has ever given.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="d3583687-d863-4277-8416-67a8f82c3192" title="The flagship Meridian above Oro" words="0-32, 54-71, 299-306" -->

The human flagship facing eleven thousand Swarm ships above the gas giant Oro and its colonised moons.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="db1e3501-d35a-493a-b853-ebb258a90946" title="War" words="80-93, 129-149, 353-373" -->

The war that threatens after human ships destroy a Swarm nursery fleet, with every torpedo already locked on the envoy's shuttle.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="fb840c1c-61b9-4124-859b-559a7aef454d" title="Sacrifice" words="216-231, 270-273, 374-407" -->

Varn gives up her ship, her command and her life with her daughter so that the peace will hold.

<!-- cell: plot id="d640355b-aba7-4f82-bc53-897767434906" title="The hostage" words="0-32, 97-119, 129-166, 209-247, 263-269, 270-273, 345-373, 374-407" -->

The Swarm demands a guarantee for the killing at Kesh; Varn offers herself as hostage, the envoy accepts, and the Swarm fleet withdraws; years later the peace still holds.

<!-- cell: plot id="6da434f6-4048-4cd0-9124-0c596286137d" title="Dace and the guns" words="72-93, 94-96, 181-192, 274-280, 281-306, 307-344" -->

Her first officer wants to fire on the envoy and refuses to let her go; Varn relieves him and hands him the ship, and he chooses to stand the weapons down.
